Transient urticarial rash (hives) from suspected mild food intolerance, resolving spontaneously
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Pruritic migrating wheals, Central pallor, Spontaneous rapid resolution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-047
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of a widespread, intensely pruritic rash requiring immediate assessment to rule out impending systemic anaphylaxis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden onset of rapidly spreading hives following food ingestion. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to evaluate airway patency, confirm hemodynamic stability, and rule out early signs of an impending severe allergic reaction which can cause fatal glottic edema or hypotensive shock.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I ate some new takeout food and now my whole body is covered in intensely itchy red welts.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes the sudden eruption of pruritic raised erythematous wheals with central pallor, appearing 30 minutes after eating a novel food item. Reports rapid migration and changing morphology of lesions over hours. Notes the transient nature of the rash, with individual hives lasting less than 24 hours before fading and reappearing elsewhere. Complete absence of lip, tongue, or pharyngeal swelling. Denies shortness of breath, wheezing, throat tightness, severe gastrointestinal cramping, or vomiting.
Functional Status: Fully independent, highly distracted by pruritus.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxiety regarding potential progression to a severe breathing emergency.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 84 | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, scratching actively, breathing comfortably on room air.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals widespread pruritic migrating wheals with central pallor. Erythematous borders blanch upon direct pressure. Diagnostic triad confirmed: pruritic migrating wheals, central pallor, and spontaneous rapid resolution of older lesions. Normal work of breathing without stridor or wheezing. Oropharynx is clear without mucosal edema or uvular swelling. Normal hemodynamic profile without hypotensive shock.
Environmental Observations: Suspect food container on the kitchen bench.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has not taken any antihistamines prior to visit.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Transient urticarial rash (hives) from suspected mild food intolerance, resolving spontaneously - Status: Improving
Differentials:

Systemic Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal blood pressure, absence of airway swelling or stridor, and lack of severe vomiting or systemic collapse.

Erythema Multiforme - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Rapid migration of lesions (EM lesions are fixed for days) and absence of classic target lesions.

Viral Exanthem - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intensely pruritic wheals with central pallor rather than a diffuse maculopapular eruption, and absence of viral prodrome.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden development of widespread urticaria following a food exposure strongly mimics the initial cutaneous phase of anaphylaxis. Given the potential for rapid progression to life-threatening airway obstruction, clinical assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period. Immediate, face-to-face evaluation confirmed an intact, uncompromised airway and stable hemodynamics, safely categorizing the reaction as isolated cutaneous urticaria. This averted an unnecessary emergency department transfer while ensuring appropriate histamine blockade.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administration of an immediate dose of a fast-acting, second-generation oral antihistamine (e.g., cetirizine). Advice on cool showers to vasoconstrict dermal vessels and reduce itch.
Positive Behavioral Change: Take a photo of the ingredients list of the suspected food immediately, creating a concrete record to help identify the specific trigger for future avoidance and discussion with an allergist.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if hives persist daily for more than 6 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop sudden difficulty breathing, voice changes, swelling of the lips or tongue, or if you feel dizzy and lightheaded.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is isolated cutaneous urticaria; future reactions to the same food could potentially be more severe.
Clinical Course: The hives will migrate and fluctuate but should completely resolve within 24 to 48 hours with regular antihistamine use.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you feel a lump in your throat that prevents swallowing, or if you develop severe, sudden stomach cramps and vomiting.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: An ingredient in the food triggered immune cells in your skin to release massive amounts of histamine, causing fluid to leak into the skin (hives) and causing the severe itch. The antihistamine blocks this chemical.
Actionable Steps: Take the antihistamine daily until the hives completely stop. Avoid the suspected food entirely. Use a cool compress on the worst areas.

